  "question": "Library management system aims to enhance library management efficiency, optimize resource allocation, and improve user experience. As an important venue for knowledge dissemination and cultural exchange, library management involves various aspects such as personnel information, book information, and borrowing information. With the increasing volume of library collections and diverse borrowing demands, traditional manual management methods are inadequate to meet modern management requirements, necessitating a comprehensive, computerized management platform.\n\nThe library management system will efficiently manage and maintain the following content:\n\nPersonnel management: store personnel information within the library, including professors and students' personal data, facilitating the management of borrowing records and contact information.  \nBook management: manage the library's book information, ensure effective registration of books, update publication information, and manage book copies.  \nBorrowing management: record borrower and borrowing information, manage the start time, end time, and return status of each borrowing event, support borrowing history queries.  \nReturn management: record the return date and copy information of books, timely update books' borrowable status, prevent borrowing conflicts and overdue situations.  \nQuery and statistics functions: provide powerful query and statistics functions, help administrators quickly query borrowing situations, promptly identify overdue unreturned books, optimize the flow and borrowing management of book resources.  \nThe design goal of the system is to optimize book borrowing and returning management processes through database support, provide real-time query and statistical report functions, and enhance the operating efficiency of the library.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0315\nCREATE TABLE `personnel` (\n  `personnel_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `personnel_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `gender` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `department` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `student_number` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `phone` varchar(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`personnel_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `book` (\n  `book_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `isbn` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `author` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `publisher` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `publication_date` date DEFAULT NULL,\n  `category` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `edition` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `total_copies` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `available_copies` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `location` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`book_id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `uk_book_isbn` (`isbn`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `book_copy` (\n  `copy_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `book_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `copy_code` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'available',\n  `shelf_location` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `acquired_at` date DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`copy_id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `uk_book_copy_code` (`copy_code`),\n  KEY `book_copy_ibfk_1` (`book_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `book_copy_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`book_id`) REFERENCES `book` (`book_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `borrowing` (\n  `borrowing_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `personnel_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `copy_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `borrow_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `due_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `return_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `return_status` varchar(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'borrowed',\n  `remarks` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`borrowing_id`),\n  KEY `borrowing_ibfk_1` (`personnel_id`),\n  KEY `borrowing_ibfk_2` (`copy_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `borrowing_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`personnel_id`) REFERENCES `personnel` (`personnel_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `borrowing_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`copy_id`) REFERENCES `book_copy` (`copy_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `return_record` (\n  `return_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `borrowing_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `copy_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `return_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `condition_note` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `processed_by` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`return_id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `uk_return_borrowing` (`borrowing_id`),\n  KEY `return_record_ibfk_2` (`copy_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `return_record_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`borrowing_id`) REFERENCES `borrowing` (`borrowing_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `return_record_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`copy_id`) REFERENCES `book_copy` (`copy_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
